From: GPU lane (diagnosis 2026-11-21); rebased to the real CI baseline by baseband lane 2026-11-21
Subject: 0504 sweet_ufs pl_thread: make pandora_reboot_after independent of the log file

The bounded reset used to be checked at the bottom of pl_thread's loop, after pl_flush(), and one
failed UFS write returned from the logging thread entirely, silently cancelling the reset for the
rest of the boot: the phone stays in iOS with USB enumerated until it is reset by hand.

2026-11-21: this actually happened.  A 30-minute trial ran from 08:51 and the phone did not come
back for eight hours; every lane waiting for the device was blocked behind it.  The ceiling the
user has now set for any trial is 180 s, and a cancelled deadline makes any ceiling meaningless.

Why this version: the earlier 0504 (and its CarPlay rebase) were written against a body that ends
the deadline branch with the orderly-shutdown path, and they do not apply to the tree the CI
actually compiles -- `git apply` fails at bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c:2219/2224 because this baseline still
uses pl_psci_reset().  Re-expressed here on the body that is really there: the deadline block moves
to the top of the loop, and a write failure only clears pl_ready instead of returning.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c b/bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c
index fe9aa8d..89ef8d0 100644
--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c
@@ -2206,6 +2206,34 @@ pl_thread(__unused void *arg, __unused wait_result_t wr)
 	nanoseconds_to_absolutetime((uint64_t)iostats * NSEC_PER_SEC, &io_every);
 	for (;;) {
 		(void)tsleep(&pl_chan, PZERO, "pandoralog", hz * (int)secs);
+		/*
+		 * 0504: the bounded reset must not be hostage to the log file.
+		 *
+		 * The deadline used to be checked at the bottom of this loop, after pl_flush(), and
+		 * one failed write returned from the thread entirely.  pl_flush() ->
+		 * ufs_write_blocks() -> ufs_scsi() is a blocking disk write that can return
+		 * ETIMEDOUT, so a single transient failure silently cancelled pandora_reboot_after
+		 * for the rest of the boot: the phone stayed in iOS with USB enumerated until
+		 * somebody reset it by hand (2026-11-21, eight hours).
+		 *
+		 * So the deadline is now checked first, before anything touches the disk, and a
+		 * write failure only stops the logging -- never the reset.
+		 */
+		if (limit && mach_absolute_time() - start >= limit) {
+			printf("PANDORA: pandora_reboot_after=%u reached, resetting\n", reboot_after);
+			pandora_amp_final("pandora_reboot_after");      /* 0298: the speaker amps down first */
+			lck_mtx_lock(&sweet_ufs_lock);
+			(void)pl_flush();
+			lck_mtx_unlock(&sweet_ufs_lock);
+			pl_psci_reset();
+		}
+		/*
+		 * Logging has stopped (a write failed once): keep waking up so the deadline above
+		 * keeps firing, but stay off the disk entirely from here on.
+		 */
+		if (!pl_ready) {
+			continue;
+		}
 		lck_mtx_lock(&sweet_ufs_lock);
 		uint64_t now = mach_absolute_time();
 		if (io_every && now - io_last >= io_every) {
@@ -2223,15 +2251,6 @@ pl_thread(__unused void *arg, __unused wait_result_t wr)
 		if (error) {
 			printf("PANDORA LOG: write failed (%d), log stopped\n", error);
 			pl_ready = false;
-			return;
-		}
-		if (limit && mach_absolute_time() - start >= limit) {
-			printf("PANDORA: pandora_reboot_after=%u reached, resetting\n", reboot_after);
-			pandora_amp_final("pandora_reboot_after");      /* 0298: the speaker amps down first */
-			lck_mtx_lock(&sweet_ufs_lock);
-			(void)pl_flush();
-			lck_mtx_unlock(&sweet_ufs_lock);
-			pl_psci_reset();
 		}
 	}
 }
